module gunbc.runner_slot_allocation

import std.types { Int, String, List, Bool }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size, byte_size_count, HardwareThreadCount, hardware_thread_count, hardware_thread_count_value }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Terminal, SingleAuthority, Scaffold }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.fleet_intent_network {
  operator_host_srv1, operator_host_srv2, operator_host_srv3, operator_host_srv4,
}
import gunbc.ci_failure_class { FloorOutcome, NoFailure, FloorFailed, Structural, floor_outcome_blocks_merge }

type RunnerSlotLabels {
  github_labels: List<String>
}

type RunnerSlotDesired {
  memory_max: ByteSize
  memory_swap_max: ByteSize
  memory_high: ByteSize
  tasks_max: Int
  cpu_weight: Int
  labels: RunnerSlotLabels
}

type HostRunnerSlotAllocation {
  host: HostIdentity
  slots_per_host: Int
  cores_per_slot: HardwareThreadCount
  runner_slice_cap: ByteSize
  per_slot: RunnerSlotDesired
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_authority_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "Single authority for fleet runner-slot desired allocation (operator rulings 2026-07-10 superseding the 2026-07-05 brief; MemoryHigh-emit + swap-provisioning 2026-07-11; srv3 host-standup converge 2026-07-21; PER-HOST WIDTH + 14GiB slot 2026-08-05; srv4 disk row-pin retired + apply-time preflight 2026-08-06). Fleet-uniform per-slot row: 8 cores · 14GiB MemoryMax · 13GiB MemoryHigh (the authored max−1GiB relation, its own emitted knob per the 2026-07-11 ruling — a hit at 14GiB spills to swap after throttling at 13) · 32GiB MemorySwapMax · TasksMax 16384 (the 2026-06-21 incident REPAIR value restored by operator ruling 2026-08-08 on PR 8045: TasksMax=512 WAS the diagnosed EAGAIN/process-creation outage value, and the earlier 512 row here inverted that diagnosis; any lower derived envelope value must be separately accepted and never below live task demand) · cpu_weight 100. PER-HOST width: MEMORY admits 5/5/7/7 for srv1/srv2/srv3/srv4; COMMITTED width is the minimum over allocation-time axes — srv1/srv2/srv3 commit 5/5/7; srv4 commits 7 at memory with disk DiskWidthUnconstrained at allocation (apply-time gate gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_width_disk_preflight; width_is_a_minimum_over_axes_note) — fleet total 24. GUARANTEED mode is now STRUCTURAL rather than checked — runner_slice_cap is DERIVED as slots × MemoryMax instead of being a second literal, so Σ per-slot MemoryMax ≤ slice_cap holds by construction and an oversubscribed slice has no representation here (DESIGN 5 construction-over-validation; the prior 80GiB literal was kept coherent with 5 × 16GiB by hand, which the old note admitted when it called them 'coherent by construction at these values' — a coincidence maintained by an author, not a derivation). The real remaining constraint is CONSERVATION against host RAM, which is not this module's to check: gunbc.ci_runner_placement holds it, now against OS-usable RAM with the per-host session slice and per-host fixed overhead charged. Per-axis targets are operator-set; never reconcile this row toward live readback (live is not automatically truth — the 32-vs-0 swap fork existed precisely because the apply was assumed rather than readback-proven). Downstream: gunbc.ci_floor_measurement (declared vs live cap fork), gunbc.ci_runner_placement, gunbc.host_converge, gunbc.host_standup (prefix:host-converge + P1:runner-deploy-slot), gunbc.runner_slot_enforcement, gunbc.fleet_show_effective_read. ctrl applies host-side; gunbc emits converge — never apply from this module."
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note: String = "WHY 14GiB AND NOT 16, measured rather than preferred (operator ruling 2026-08-05). 186 floor-job cgroup peaks from 2026-08-05 plus five sampled days back to 2026-07-16, read from the [calibration] floor_peak_post receipts the ci job emits: successful runs peak at p50 10.32GiB and max 10.96GiB, and NOT ONE run in the sample reached the 16GiB MemoryMax. Four of 186 reached 15.0GiB, which is the 15GiB MemoryHigh exactly — those are throttle pins, censored lower bounds rather than demand, and they are why the cap does not fall further.\n\nTHE FLOOR UNDER IT IS ALREADY MEASURED AND IT IS 12GiB: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget in this module, enforced by claim_executor as an arm-time refusal. MemoryHigh is what the run-time governor arms against, so MemoryHigh is the axis that number constrains — 13GiB leaves exactly 1GiB of margin over it, and clears the worst p90 in the three-week sample (12.94GiB on 2026-07-30). Going to 13/12 to buy an eighth slot on the managed hosts would put the throttle line ON the measured refusal floor with zero margin, and the 2026-07-30 distribution says that day would have spent most of its runs in swap.\n\nWHAT MOVING 16 TO 14 IS NOT: it is not oversubscription and does not touch the guaranteed-mode property. Every slot still owns its ceiling outright. The ceilings are simply sized closer to what the workload was measured to want, which is what freed the width."

data gunbc_runner_slot_width_ruling_note: String = "WHY srv3/srv4 GET 7 AND srv1/srv2 STAY AT 5 — the width difference is DERIVED from two per-host facts, not granted by preference. First, sessions: gunbc.session_residency host_session_residency places interactive session containers on srv1 and srv2 and on neither managed host, so srv1/srv2 owe a sessions reservation out of the same host RAM and srv3/srv4 do not. Second, fixed overhead: gunbc.ci_floor_measurement carries a measured upper bound for the managed hosts and an operator allowance for the legacy pair, which differ by 10.4GiB per host.\n\nRun the conservation arithmetic with both charged and the MEMORY admissions fall out — srv3/srv4 admit 7 slots at 14GiB and refuse 8 (an eighth would need 112GiB against ~111.6GiB available, over by less than a gibibyte), srv1/srv2 admit 5 and refuse 6. Neither number is authored here as a target; both are the largest width gunbc.ci_runner_placement's wall will pass, and if either per-host fact is corrected the admissible width moves with it.\n\nMEMORY ADMISSION IS NOT THE FULL STORY FOR DISK ON srv4. This note explains the memory axis; gunbc_runner_slots_per_host derives committed width as the minimum over allocation-time axes. srv4's disk row-pin is retired: host_disk_admitted_width returns DiskWidthUnconstrained and srv4 commits 7 like srv3 at allocation; insufficient or unobservable disk at apply is refused by gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_width_disk_preflight rather than lowering allocation to 5.\n\nTHE SESSIONS FACT IS RESIDENCY, AND SAYING SO HERE IS LOAD-BEARING. An earlier revision of this note derived the same widths from gunbc.build_cache_instance instead — whether a host carried an InteractiveSessionCompiles cache instance — which merely CORRELATED with sessions living there. That proxy is the defect this change removes, and gunbc.session_residency why_this_is_not_derived_from_the_build_cache_roster_note records how close it came: the session cache on srv1 and srv2 was found provably dead on 2026-08-06 and deleted, so dropping its now-meaningless roster rows would have stopped charging both hosts their reservation and admitted 7 slots each on a wall returning true. A note still reasoning from the roster would invite exactly that edit, which is why it is corrected rather than left as harmless prose.\n\nTHE FLEET-UNIFORM ROW IS WHY THIS COULD NOT BE EXPRESSED BEFORE. gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation took a host and then read three globals, so 'this host is different' had nowhere to live and the only way to raise srv3/srv4 was to raise all four — which on srv1/srv2 would have overcommitted by 17GiB against a wall that could not see the sessions it was overcommitting against."

// 🟡 REGISTERED FOLLOW-UP — task-budget envelope model (operator ruling on PR 8045, 2026-08-09).
// tasks_max=16384 below is APPROVED as an EMERGENCY PARITY ROLLBACK ONLY: it is not accepted as
// a derived value, and RunnerSlotDesired.tasks_max is not accepted as the model home. The
// follow-up model must distinguish THREE task budgets, each derived from one measured envelope
// (concurrent-build admission × compiler/thread fan-out): (1) runner-slot task budget; (2) shared
// compile-pool task budget — sccache cache-miss compiles run as children of the SERVER'S cgroup,
// not the requester's (gunbc.build_cache_instance placement axis), so the pool owns their task
// count exactly as compile_pool owns their memory; (3) host-parent reserve — with the conservation
// law sum(slots) + pool + reserve <= parent ancestor limit. Dissolve-on: that model landing, at
// which point this row's tasks_max derives rather than being authored. Do not build it in PR 8045.
fn gunbc_runner_slot_desired() -> RunnerSlotDesired {
  RunnerSlotDesired {
    memory_max: byte_size(15032385536),
    memory_swap_max: byte_size(34359738368),
    memory_high: byte_size(13958643712),
    tasks_max: 16384,
    cpu_weight: 100,
    labels: RunnerSlotLabels { github_labels: ["self-hosted", "linux", "arm64", "gunbc-heavy"] }
  }
}

data gunbc_runner_slots_per_host_unknown_note: String = "Unknown hosts refuse rather than defaulting. A width is a claim about a specific machine's RAM, and handing an unclassified host the managed pair's 7 would be the same borrowed-premise failure the fixed-overhead fallback carries (gunbc.ci_floor_measurement gunbc_ci_host_fixed_overhead_unknown_host_note) — worse here, because width multiplies the per-slot ceiling. Zero slots is the fail-closed answer: gunbc.ci_runner_placement's any_unfit arm already refuses a plan that admits zero runners on an enrolled host, so an unclassified host produces a located PlanUnsound rather than a silent five."

data width_is_a_minimum_over_axes_note: String = "A HOST'S WIDTH AT ALLOCATION IS THE MINIMUM OVER ALLOCATION-TIME CONSTRAINTS. Until this row existed gunbc_runner_slots_per_host returned the MEMORY verdict under a name that read as the full commitment — the defect that made srv4's roster and prose disagree when a disk row-pin silently lowered allocation to 5 while runner_host_deploy named seven slots.\n\nCollapsing 'one constraint admits this width' into 'this host should run this width' is the same conflation gunbc.ci_floor_measurement refuses for overhead: an axis nobody resolved is not an axis that permits everything. Memory admission and disk admission remain separate answers at allocation; gunbc_runner_slots_per_host takes their minimum when disk admits a numeric cap, otherwise memory alone.\n\nsrv4's historical five-slot disk row-pin is retired (operator 2026-08-06): host_disk_admitted_width returns DiskWidthUnconstrained citing apply-time gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_width_disk_preflight, so srv4 commits 7 at allocation like srv3 — disk no longer derives a lower minimum here. Insufficient or unobservable disk at apply refuses rather than absorbing to width 5 (gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4_admission_note).\n\nHONEST RESIDUAL: srv1-3 still have DiskWidthUnconstrained without a measured disk width — status quo, not a new widen. Dissolve-on for allocation-time disk on all hosts is a per-host disk-headroom term folded into runner_deployment_plan; srv4's apply-time gate remains even after that lands."

fn host_memory_admitted_width(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv1 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv2 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv3 as String) {
    7
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv4 as String) {
    7
  } else {
    0
  }
}

type HostDiskWidthConstraint
  = DiskAdmitsWidth { slots: Int, provenance: String }
  | DiskWidthUnconstrained { reason: String }

fn host_disk_admitted_width(host: HostIdentity) -> HostDiskWidthConstraint {
  if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv4 as String) {
    DiskWidthUnconstrained {
      reason: "srv4 disk width is gated at apply-time by gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_width_disk_preflight (operator 2026-08-06); runner_host_deploy runner_count names materialization target 7, not a row pin that silently caps allocation"
    }
  } else {
    DiskWidthUnconstrained {
      reason: "no per-host disk cap is declared for this host and no disk-headroom term is modeled, so its disk admits no stated width and does not lower the minimum: an unmeasured axis, not a measured all-clear"
    }
  }
}

fn gunbc_runner_slots_per_host(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  let by_memory = host_memory_admitted_width(host: host)
  match host_disk_admitted_width(host: host) {
    DiskAdmitsWidth { slots: d, provenance: _ } => if d < by_memory { d } else { by_memory }
    DiskWidthUnconstrained { reason: _ } => by_memory
  }
}

fn gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot() -> HardwareThreadCount {
  hardware_thread_count(count: 8)
}

fn gunbc_runner_slice_cap_declared(host: HostIdentity) -> ByteSize {
  gunbc_runner_slot_demand_sum_memory_max(
    slots: gunbc_runner_slots_per_host(host: host),
    per_slot: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max
  )
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_demand_sum_memory_max(slots: Int, per_slot: ByteSize) -> ByteSize {
  byte_size(byte_size_count(b: per_slot) * slots)
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice(slots: Int, per_slot: ByteSize, slice_cap: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_demand_sum_memory_max(slots: slots, per_slot: per_slot)) <=
    byte_size_count(b: slice_cap)
}

fn gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(host: HostIdentity) -> HostRunnerSlotAllocation {
  let per_slot = gunbc_runner_slot_desired()
  let slots = gunbc_runner_slots_per_host(host: host)
  HostRunnerSlotAllocation {
    host: host,
    slots_per_host: slots,
    cores_per_slot: gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot(),
    runner_slice_cap: gunbc_runner_slice_cap_declared(host: host),
    per_slot: per_slot
  }
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv1: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv1
)

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv2: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv2
)

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv3: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv3
)

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4_admission_note: String = "srv4 commits 7 slots at the memory axis (no interactive sessions, per gunbc_runner_slot_width_ruling_note). Disk binding is NOT folded into gunbc_runner_slots_per_host anymore: the historical five-slot row pin is retired, and apply-time disk preflight in gunbc.runner_lifecycle refuses insufficient or unobservable headroom rather than absorbing to width 5. srv4_runner_host_deploy.runner_count and this roster both name 7 as the materialization target; vivid-eagle-131's apply_wet path must run runner_width_disk_preflight before ADD on srv4."

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv4
)

fn gunbc_committed_runner_slot_allocations() -> List<HostRunnerSlotAllocation> {
  [
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv1,
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv2,
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv3,
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4,
  ]
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_enforcement_expected_cap() -> ByteSize {
  gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max
}

type RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation
  = RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured {
      runner_unit: String,
      memory_max: ByteSize,
      memory_high: ByteSize,
      probed_at: String,
    }
  | RunnerSlotEffectiveCapUnmeasured

data gunbc_runner_slot_effective_readback_refusal_note: String = "EFFECTIVE KERNEL CAPS ARE NOT THIS MODULE'S INPUT. gunbc_runner_slot_desired is the single authority for the fleet per-slot row; per-axis targets are operator-set and never reconciled toward live readback — live is not automatically truth, and the 32-vs-0 swap fork existed precisely because apply was assumed rather than readback-proven.\n\nTHE 14GiB/13GiB ROW IS OBSERVATION-DERIVED FROM A DIFFERENT SUBJECT: the 2026-08-05 floor_peak_post calibration in gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note (186 cgroup peaks, p50 10.32GiB, max 10.96GiB). That derivation is complete; a per-slot systemctl show on an unconverged host is evidence of CONVERGENCE DEBT, not a second numeric source for the declaration.\n\nsrv2-03 (gunbc_srv2_03_effective_cap_observation) is the discriminating specimen: effective MemoryMax/MemoryHigh still read 16GiB/15GiB while the declared row is 14GiB/13GiB. runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration is true; gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation is true — the repair is host_axis_caps deploy apply (author derived drop-ins, shadow revert), never editing gunbc_runner_slot_desired toward the observation. Until apply lands, memory_governor arms against the live cgroup on that slot (15GiB throttle), while placement and ci_budget_tree size against the declared 13GiB — the honest residual in gunbc_ci_runner_slot_memory_max_live_source. APPLY HAS A LIVE CONSUMER THAT DOES NOT FIT: gunbc_srv2_03_converge_floor_fit_note — floor T5 entry RSS on srv2-03 exceeds both declared caps, so blind converge OOM-kills the floor walk; coordinate with the floor lane before apply on that host."

data gunbc_srv2_03_effective_cap_observation: RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation = RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured {
  runner_unit: "actions-runner@srv2-03.service",
  memory_max: byte_size(17179869184),
  memory_high: byte_size(16106127360),
  probed_at: "2026-08-16",
}

data gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_rss: ByteSize = byte_size(15773273292)

data gunbc_srv2_03_converge_floor_fit_note: String = "CONVERGING srv2-03 TO THE DECLARED ROW WOULD OOM-KILL THE CI FLOOR ON THAT HOST. The slot survives today only because it is drifted upward to 16GiB/15GiB effective (gunbc_srv2_03_effective_cap_observation); applying host_axis_caps deploy converge to the declared 14GiB/13GiB row restores the envelope the 2026-08-05 floor_peak calibration sized, but the floor itself no longer fits it on srv2-03. Measured floor RSS at T5 entry on this host: 14.69GiB (gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_rss = 15773273292 bytes) — 0.69GiB above declared memory.max (15032385536) and 1.69GiB above declared memory.high (13958643712), so memory.max would OOM-kill the floor walk the moment the host converges. This is NOT a reason to soften gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation: the declaration is derived from 186 floor-job cgroup peaks and the floor is over budget, which is the floor lane's problem (sleek-moth-351 / PR 8283: fit in 14GiB), not the row's. It IS a reason whoever lands the apply must not be surprised by immediate floor OOM — coordinate with the floor lane before blind converge on srv2-03."

fn srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_exceeds_declared_memory_max() -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_rss) > byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max)
}

fn srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_exceeds_declared_memory_high() -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_rss) > byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_high)
}

fn srv2_03_converge_would_oom_floor_at_declared_cap() -> Bool {
  srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_exceeds_declared_memory_max()
    && srv2_03_floor_t5_entry_exceeds_declared_memory_high()
}

fn runner_slot_effective_memory_max_drifts_from_declaration(effective: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: effective) != byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max)
}

fn runner_slot_effective_memory_high_drifts_from_declaration(effective: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: effective) != byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_high)
}

fn runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration(obs: RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation) -> Bool {
  match obs {
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured { runner_unit: _, memory_max: max, memory_high: high, probed_at: _ } =>
      runner_slot_effective_memory_max_drifts_from_declaration(effective: max)
        || runner_slot_effective_memory_high_drifts_from_declaration(effective: high)
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapUnmeasured => false
  }
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation(obs: RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation) -> Bool {
  match obs {
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured { runner_unit: _, memory_max: _, memory_high: _, probed_at: _ } =>
      runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration(obs: obs)
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapUnmeasured => false
  }
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_wall_oracle_note: String = "WHAT THIS WALL USED TO ASSERT WAS ITS OWN INPUT. Three of its five conjuncts compared gunbc_runner_slot_desired's fields to byte literals retyped from that same declaration twenty lines above, in the same file — measure() == measure() with the equality maintained by hand, which the 2026-08-01 test-oracle ruling names exactly: automate the literal's update and the assertion collapses to nothing, so the manual update WAS the assertion's entire content. It could only ever fail when someone edited one copy and forgot the other, and it would have gone red on this change for that reason and no other.\n\nThe conjuncts that replace them are relations between INDEPENDENTLY derived quantities, so each can fail for a reason worth knowing. The max−1GiB relation is the authored MemoryHigh law from the 2026-07-11 ruling and it is checkable without naming either number. The MemoryHigh ≥ floor conjunct is the load-bearing one and it is NEW: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget is a MEASURED refusal threshold (12GiB, from the doomed-witness receipts below) that claim_executor enforces at arm time, and MemoryHigh is precisely the budget the run-time governor arms against — so shrinking the slot cap below it would ship a fleet on which every floor run refuses itself. Nothing connected those two rows before; the 14GiB ruling is what made the distance between them small enough to matter.\n\nGuaranteed mode is deliberately NOT asserted here any more. runner_slice_cap is derived as slots × MemoryMax, so Σ ≤ cap is true by construction and a check of it would be the vacuous restatement DESIGN 5 says to prefer construction over. gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice survives as the general relation for callers holding two independent numbers."

fn gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_wall_holds() -> Bool {
  let per_slot = gunbc_runner_slot_desired()
  let gibibyte_bytes = 1073741824
  (hardware_thread_count_value(t: gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot()) == 8)
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) + gibibyte_bytes == byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) >= byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_swap_max) > byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && all(
      gunbc_committed_runner_slot_allocations(),
      a =>
        a.slots_per_host > 0
          && gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice(
               slots: a.slots_per_host,
               per_slot: a.per_slot.memory_max,
               slice_cap: a.runner_slice_cap
             )
    )
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_live_cap_dissolve_on: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.runner_slot_allocation",
    decl_name: "gunbc_runner_slot_desired",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

